I fixed the bug, but issue #665 is not closed: my time ran out before the land's verdict came back and before my local full gates finished.

**The fix (commit c416e3a7, queued to land on main):** I took the issue's first option. `python` is removed from `mcpHostLanguages` in `pkg/internal/common/parser/mcp_validate.go`, so it is treated like TypeScript, Swift and C#. Python only generates clients and already refuses to generate servers, so it can never host MCP tools and the MCP-6 check had nothing to protect against. The check itself stays in place for a future server language that lacks MCP support. `TestAutoExpose_Mcp6LanguageGate` now accepts python and tests the refusal with a stand-in language. The test failed before the fix and passes after it. I also updated the MCP-6 paragraph in `docs/USER.md`.

**What I verified:**
- **Python client build:** a Python build of `example/proto`'s `notesclient`, which calls `notesapi.NotesApi`, fails with the MCP-6 error on main and generates after the fix. The same is true on release/2.0.
- **Python host build:** a Python build that tries to host `notesapi` is still refused, by Python's own client-only rule.
- **Local `just gates`:** protoc-check, soundness and test passed. current-generation was still running when time ran out, so I stopped it; the full local run is not confirmed.
- **Not checked:** `cross-test/runtime-smoke/run.sh --consumer python`. On main it still fails at setup on `platform/health.proto`, which #657 is fixing.

**Release:** I tagged nothing on main, because main has taken features since v2.6.1 (#321, #266). Also, the newest 2.6 release is v2.6.11, not v2.6.1 as the run's instructions said, so the patch can't be v2.6.2. Every release line from 2.0 to 2.6 has the bug. Each has a draft backport PR, and each PR names that line's next patch tag (v2.6.12 for 2.6) to cut once it merges. The parser tests pass on every branch.

| Line | PR |
|---|---|
| 2.0 | #691 |
| 2.1 | #692 |
| 2.2 | #693 |
| 2.3 | #694 |
| 2.4 | #695 |
| 2.5 | #696 |
| 2.6 | #697 |

No service handlers were touched, so there was no trace propagation to check.

I posted a progress comment on #665 and checkpointed the branch. The manager still lands the queued commit after this run. The next run needs to read the land's verdict and the gate set it ran, take the backports out of draft, post the final comment and close the issue.
